\section{Cross-VLA Analysis: Model Capability Dependency}
\label{sec:cross_vla}

\looseness=-1 We evaluate the CRM degradation pattern on $\pi_{0.5}$~\cite{black2025pi05} (3.6B, flow matching), where near-saturated baselines reach 94--98\% (Table~\ref{tab:pi05}). The same K-matched design ($K{=}2$, $\varepsilon{=}0.2$, shared CRM architecture) is used. Unconditional CRM correction degrades all four suites: Long suffers $-$17.0\,pp ($94.0{\to}77.0$\%, the largest single-suite drop across both VLAs), Goal $-$14.0\,pp, Object $-$6.0\,pp, and Spatial $-$2.0\,pp. As on SmolVLA, the CRM is trained on three suites and Long is out-of-distribution, amplifying the harm. The degradation on all four suites of both VLAs is consistent with the core thesis: when most steps are already near-optimal, learned correction noise has proportionally greater impact, and this pattern generalizes across model scale.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Cross-VLA degradation analysis: $\pi_{0.5}$ (3.6B) on LIBERO. Near-saturated baselines (94--98\%) are degraded by unconditional CRM correction on all four suites, with Long suffering $-$17.0\,pp (the largest drop across both VLAs).}
\label{tab:pi05}

